.TH FERRET 1 "2026-07-03" "ferret 0.1.0-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ferret, frt \- fast, byte-for-byte GNU find(1) clone
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ferret
.RI [ path ...]
.RI [ expression ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ferret
is a from-scratch C reimplementation of GNU
.BR find (1).
For a given expression and filesystem state its output matches GNU find 4.10.0
byte for byte, and it is faster on the workloads measured.
.PP
For the expression language (predicates, actions, and operators) see the GNU
.BR find (1)
manual; ferret matches it. The tests documented below are ferret extensions
that GNU find does not provide.
.SH FERRET EXTENSIONS
.TP
.BI \-contains " string"
True if
.I file
is a regular file whose contents contain
.I string
as a byte substring. Non-regular files never match. The match is binary-safe
(NUL bytes are matched literally) and the file is streamed in a bounded window,
so memory use does not scale with file size. The cost model ranks this test
above a stat, so cheaper predicates run first and content is read only for files
that already pass them \(em e.g.
.B \-name '*.c' \-mtime \-1 \-contains TODO
reads only recently\-modified C files.
.TP
.BI \-icontains " string"
Like
.BR \-contains ,
but the match folds ASCII case (locale\-independent).
.TP
.BI \-\-ferret\-threads " n"
Worker count for the parallel stat pass (a global option, given before the
expression).
.B 1
forces a serial walk,
.B 0
forces all CPUs, and
.I n
sets an explicit count. Omitted, ferret decides per query: it engages the pool
only for a stat\-bound, physical (\fB\-P\fR) walk with no
.B \-name
filter, where every entry would be stat'd anyway. Output is byte\-identical for
any worker count.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
all operands processed successfully;
.B 1
an error occurred (a bad command line or a runtime failure on a file).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR find (1)
.SH AUTHORS
The ferret authors.
